WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:45.530
Breaking the fourth wall is one of the most
unique thematic devices at the disposal of

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:49.440
a filmmaker. Most cinematic techniques pull
us into a story and make us forget we’re

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.920
watching a film, but breaking the fourth wall
does the opposite. It reminds us we’re actually

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.740
an audience member watching what’s happening
on screen.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.720
The technique can contribute to

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.780
the visual storytelling of a film in many
different ways depending on how it’s presented.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.610
To really understand the power of breaking
the fourth wall, we have to look at its inception

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.030
in theatrical performance.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.259
A formal definition for breaking the fourth

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:29.759
wall would be a dramatic technique in a work
of fiction where characters display an awareness

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:40.259
that they are in such a work. The technique
goes as far back as Ancient Greece and continued

00:01:40.259 --> 00:01:44.590
through Shakespeare’s time and well into
postmodern theater, especially musicals. The

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.289
term first arose after the use of Box Sets
became popular in the 1800’s when sets were

00:01:49.289 --> 00:01:53.759
constructed with three walls, the fourth wall
being the imaginary wall between the audience

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:58.259
and the performers on stage. So whenever a
character acknowledged the audience’s presence,

00:01:58.259 --> 00:01:59.379
the fourth wall was broken.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.900
The technique has been used since the silent

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:12.450
era, and Charlie Chaplin played around extensively
with fourth wall breaks in his films. With

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.980
the introduction of sound and the popularity
of the musical genre, fourth wall breaks became

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.290
more common to contribute to the spectacle
of film, and over the next 70 years, fourth

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.069
wall breaks have made their way into a variety
of film genres with a variety of thematic effects.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.780
The most popular use of this technique is for comedy

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.440
and the comedy stands out by creating some of the most memorable punch lines in cinematic history.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.040
There’s something really surprising and hilarious about reminding us

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:04.790
that we’re in the middle of watching a film.
No matter how many times I watch a movie break

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:14.590
the fourth wall, the joke can still catch
me by surprise. The joke has to step out of

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.540
the film to deliver the punch line, and there’s
really no other type of joke like it. Every

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:23.019
other joke is enclosed within the imaginary
world of the art form. Taking that step out

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:27.259
of a movie is what makes a good fourth wall
joke so satisfying and creative.

00:03:35.249 --> 00:03:39.239
I do want to note that breaking the fourth wall doesn’t
automatically make you a comedic genius. The

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:43.120
set up and delivery of a fourth wall break
has to be at least somewhat surprising or

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.620
the joke can come off as kind of lazy.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.480
You can’t talk about breaking the fourth
wall without mentioning Mel Brooks. He broke

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:34.760
the fourth wall in the most extreme ways possible.
His films go so far as to actually have the

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.800
main characters watch themselves within the movie

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.220
While his films are funny because of their

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.120
ridiculousness, there’s definitely some
method to his madness. I like how Blazing

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.750
Saddles uses fourth wall breaks to poke fun
at the Western genre as a whole. It really

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:15.590
points out how unrealistic Hollywood films
were in their portrayal of life in the west.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:19.759
In reality, western films were nothing but
actors in a studio acting in fake towns and

00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:24.180
eventually getting into a car to drive off
the set. Blazing Saddles is a not so subtle

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.889
reminder that film as an art form has its
limitations at portraying reality. Mel Brooks

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:33.180
made a statement by pointing out the lack
of realism in the Western film. Western films

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:38.599
of the 40’s, 50’s, and 60’s almost completely
ignore racism, xenophobia, and other ugly

00:05:38.599 --> 00:05:43.169
parts of the west, and Blazing Saddles breaks
the fourth wall in an insane way to remind

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:46.429
us we’re watching a dramatized film, not
reality.

00:05:53.689 --> 00:05:56.990
Perhaps what makes breaking the fourth wall
the most intriguing is that, depending on

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:01.280
its context, the act of acknowledging the
audience can actually push us into the film

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.280
instead of pulling us out. There’s a sort
of intimacy to a character speaking directly

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.490
to us. In Road to Morocco, Bing Crosby and
Bob Hope make lighthearted jokes straight

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:13.370
to the audience and even remind us that they’re
going to end up fine on their adventure

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:27.800
We know from the casual and inviting fourth wall
breaks that this is a movie that doesn’t

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.419
take itself too seriously and will provide
light, conservative entertainment. Fourth

00:06:31.419 --> 00:06:35.470
wall breaks can be a way to welcome us into
the story, and this variation of the technique

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.370
is definitely indicative of a more casual
movie.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:51.729
A fourth wall break can make us intimate with
a character as well as with a story. There’s

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:54.789
really no stronger way to show a character’s
ulterior motives than by having them look

00:06:54.789 --> 00:07:04.099
right at us and say them. It’s a great way
to show us what’s going on in a character’s

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:08.330
head and is comparable to an aside in theater.
Frank Underwood addressing us directly is

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:16.900
the foundation for so much of the energy in
House of Cards. I love the scenes where he

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.789
says one thing to another character, and then
looks at us and reminds us what he’s really after

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:40.820
The final great effect of breaking the fourth

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:45.060
wall is to unsettle. There’s nothing scarier
than feeling safe and removed from the villain

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.120
in a movie and then having that villain look
directly at you.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:02.840
Yikes.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:07.700
Comedy, satire, intimacy, and fear can all
be accomplished just from a look to the camera.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.650
So don’t forget the great potential a fourth
wall break can have, and thanks for wa-

